WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.453 --> 00:00:01.837
- [Narrator] The largest
monument in Europe

00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:04.029
is located in Leipzig, Germany,

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:08.196
and was built on the location
Napoleon surrendered.

00:00:12.826 --> 00:00:14.519
Completed in 1913,

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:16.532
the Monument to the Battle of the Nations

00:00:16.532 --> 00:00:18.213
is named after the battle that took place

00:00:18.213 --> 00:00:21.506
100 years earlier,
between Napoleon and his allies

00:00:21.506 --> 00:00:24.581
and the United Armed
Forces of Russia, Prussia,

00:00:24.581 --> 00:00:26.461
Austria and Sweden.

00:00:26.461 --> 00:00:29.008
The fight involved around 600,000 troops

00:00:29.008 --> 00:00:31.181
from over 20 countries,
and was the largest

00:00:31.181 --> 00:00:34.619
European battle prior to World War I.

00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:37.613
At the base of the monument
is the Archangel Michael.

00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:42.326
Above him are giant stone
guards who circle the structure.

00:00:42.326 --> 00:00:45.415
Inside the ground floor,
there are eight knight statues

00:00:45.415 --> 00:00:47.672
who represent the fallen soldiers.

00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:49.695
They are joined by other smaller figures

00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:51.895
known as the Guardians of the Dead.

00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:53.740
These guardians watch over all the souls

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.676
who have been lost.

00:00:55.676 --> 00:00:57.332
All of these statues on the ground floor

00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:00.532
are shadowed by the 31-foot
giants on the second story

00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:03.083
known as the Hall of Fame.

00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:05.452
Each of these represent
the four historic qualities

00:01:05.452 --> 00:01:07.737
attributed to the German people:

00:01:07.737 --> 00:01:11.070
bravery, faith, sacrifice and fertility.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.568
During the Communist
era, when many monuments

00:01:15.568 --> 00:01:17.966
were torn down, this one survived

00:01:17.966 --> 00:01:19.813
because the structure commemorates a war

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.956
when the Russians and
Germans fought together

00:01:21.956 --> 00:01:23.873
against a common enemy.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.360
The scale of the monument inspires awe.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.742
With a height of nearly 300
feet, towering over Leipzig,

00:01:30.742 --> 00:01:34.909
and reminding all who view it
of the battles of the past.

